  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Thunderstorms will develop this afternoon and \ncontinue into the early overnight hours. Some storms could be \nsevere, with damaging winds the main threat. Localized flooding\nin the mountains.\n\nA shortwave will advance eastward into the southeast and Mid-\nAtlantic by the afternoon/evening and be the main forcing\nmechanisim for widely scattered storms. Strong surface heating \nis already ongoing across much of the forecast area, especially along\nand south of a stationary front draped roughly from Lewisburg, \nWV to Lynchburg, VA. South of this stationary front, boundary \nlayer moisture continues to increase, with upper 60s and lower \n70 dew points now showing in recent observations. This should \nset the stage for CAPE values exceeding 1500 - 2000 J/kg by the \nafternoon. Although lapse rates remain poor, strengthening mid- \nlevel flow and increasing instability will likely offset this \nand will eventually see widely scattered storms begin to develop\nwithin the next couple of hours.\n\nMain focus for storm development will initially be across\nsoutheast West Virginia and far southwest Virginia. Eventually\nwill begin to see storms along the Blue Ridge mid mid-afternoon\nforming into multicell clusters and propagating east into the\nPiedmont. A secondary line of storms may develop this evening\nthat is more closely associated with the mid-level shortwave.\nAll storms today will especially be capable of strong damaging\nwinds, with a secondary hail threat. Some local enhancement of\nstorms could also occur along the stationary front as well.\n\nStorm coverage intensity and coverage should begin to decrease\nafter 10PM tonight with some rain and possibly a few storms \nlingering through early Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Localized flooding in the mountains.\n\nRepeated rounds of storms over southeast West Virginia and far\nsouthwest Virginia may result in areas of localized flash\nflooding through this evening. Although storm motions today\nremain in the 20-30kt range, high PWAT values now beginning to\napproach 2\" and the potential for training storms gives enough \nconfidence for a Flood Watch over the aforementioned areas.",
